# Git bundles. A transfer artifact, not a build one: they are how a change gets from
# a container to a clone, they are named after whatever branch was in flight, and
# nothing ever reads them again. Nine of them rode into c697c8cd under `git add -A`.
*.bundle

# The header-dependency files PGXS does not generate and our Makefiles do
# (#1158). One per object, 36 of them after a build, directly beside the sources
# somebody types `git add -A` next to -- which is the position the nine bundles
# above were in. They are generated, so they are ignored rather than tracked.
#
# Measured on a pristine clone at 7397731: `git status --porcelain` is empty
# before a build and 36 entries after it, all `.d`, and `git check-ignore` said
# no. That makes an empty `git status` unusable as a premise, and both harness
# gates assert exactly that before they run.
#
# SAFE BECAUSE NOTHING TRACKED MATCHES: `git ls-files '*.d'` is empty. A pattern
# this broad would otherwise hide a real file the next time someone adds one.
*.d
